{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB03904 - MONDO_11292",
  "verbalization": "Urea is implicated in the pathogenesis of Dermatitis, Atopic through a series of indirect but interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Urea may act as a precursor or modulator in the treatment of Atopic Eczema, which in turn is linked to Dermatitis, Atopic. This is supported by the fact that Urea is indicated for Atopic Eczema and that both Atopic Eczema and Dermatitis, Atopic share a common indication chain involving Crisaborole and Pimecrolimus. The presence of multiple pathways connecting Urea to Atopic Eczema and then to Dermatitis, Atopic highlights a potential role of Urea in the management of atopic dermatitis, possibly through its effects on inflammatory pathways or immune modulation. The integration of these pathways suggests that Urea may influence the progression or manifestation of atopic dermatitis, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
